WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.311
Day 17 of the 31 days of QA + AI series.

00:00:04.861 --> 00:00:08.335
Golden datasets: how to label, balance and version.

00:00:08.885 --> 00:00:13.786
A small but reliable golden dataset: with a labeling guide, three splits and Git

00:00:13.786 --> 00:00:14.460
versioning.

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:20.275
Everything we measured over the last few days depends on a piece that usually gets

00:00:20.275 --> 00:00:23.634
little attention: the set of cases with their correct answer.

00:00:23.934 --> 00:00:28.543
If the labels are wrong, the best harness in the world measures the wrong thing

00:00:28.543 --> 00:00:29.127
precisely.

00:00:29.427 --> 00:00:33.133
If the set has only easy cases, any model looks excellent.

00:00:33.433 --> 00:00:37.580
And if cases leak into the prompt, the number stops meaning anything.

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:43.480
A golden dataset is that set treated with the care a measuring instrument deserves.

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:48.430
This guide covers how to gather the cases, how to label them with shared criteria,

00:00:48.430 --> 00:00:53.194
how to balance them, how to split them so you don't fool yourself and how to version

00:00:53.194 --> 00:00:53.477
them.

00:00:53.777 --> 00:00:58.378
It's craft work, and it's what most improves the quality of every later decision.

00:00:59.678 --> 00:01:01.375
To follow this guide you need:

00:01:01.925 --> 00:01:04.645
The qa-ai-lab repo, data/ folder.

00:01:05.195 --> 00:01:08.854
50 to 100 anonymized real or synthetic cases.

00:01:09.404 --> 00:01:12.263
Ideally, a second person to label a sample.

00:01:13.563 --> 00:01:14.843
This is the setup.

00:01:15.143 --> 00:01:19.360
Pause the video if you want to copy it: the full version is in the written guide.

00:01:20.660 --> 00:01:22.927
And this is the next setup file.

00:01:24.227 --> 00:01:25.262
Step 1.

00:01:25.562 --> 00:01:27.353
Three sources of cases.

00:01:27.903 --> 00:01:33.515
Collect cases from three sources: anonymized real usage, edge cases and adverse

00:01:33.515 --> 00:01:36.427
cases (filler, duplicates, empty inputs).

00:01:36.977 --> 00:01:42.061
The first source is real usage, anonymized: it shows what inputs are really like,

00:01:42.061 --> 00:01:44.132
with their typos and ambiguities.

00:01:44.432 --> 00:01:49.268
The second is the edge cases you know exist even though they rarely show up:

00:01:49.268 --> 00:01:52.260
boundary values, odd formats, very long inputs.

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:57.545
The third is adverse cases, designed to cause trouble: filler text, duplicates,

00:01:57.545 --> 00:02:00.132
empty inputs or ones in another language.

00:02:00.432 --> 00:02:05.315
A dataset built only from the first source describes the average and says nothing

00:02:05.315 --> 00:02:06.460
about the failures.

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:08.959
Step 2.

00:02:09.259 --> 00:02:10.678
The labeling guide.

00:02:11.228 --> 00:02:15.954
Write the labeling guide: definition of each label, two examples per label and what

00:02:15.954 --> 00:02:17.093
to do when in doubt.

00:02:17.643 --> 00:02:22.274
Before labeling in volume, write a short guide: the definition of each label, two

00:02:22.274 --> 00:02:25.018
examples per label and what to do when in doubt.

00:02:25.318 --> 00:02:28.293
That last part is the one most often forgotten.

00:02:28.593 --> 00:02:33.461
Without a rule for doubtful cases, each person resolves them differently and the

00:02:33.461 --> 00:02:35.469
dataset piles up inconsistencies.

00:02:35.769 --> 00:02:39.312
The guide also forces business decisions that were implicit.

00:02:39.612 --> 00:02:44.211
If writing a label's definition proves hard, the system probably can't apply it

00:02:44.211 --> 00:02:44.619
either.

00:02:45.919 --> 00:02:47.001
Step 3.

00:02:47.301 --> 00:02:49.382
Two people, twenty cases.

00:02:49.932 --> 00:02:53.023
Two people independently label a sample of 20.

00:02:53.323 --> 00:02:56.542
Where they disagree, the guide is incomplete: fix it.

00:02:57.092 --> 00:03:01.374
Take a sample of twenty cases and have two people label it separately, without

00:03:01.374 --> 00:03:02.911
seeing each other's answers.

00:03:03.211 --> 00:03:04.363
Then compare.

00:03:04.663 --> 00:03:08.358
Disagreements aren't the people's errors: they're gaps in the guide.

00:03:08.658 --> 00:03:13.397
Discuss each one, decide and update the guide with the new rule or an example.

00:03:13.697 --> 00:03:16.173
Repeat until disagreements are few.

00:03:16.473 --> 00:03:18.728
Only then is it worth labeling the rest.

00:03:19.028 --> 00:03:23.372
That agreement between people also marks the reasonable ceiling for any model.

00:03:24.672 --> 00:03:25.685
Step 4.

00:03:25.985 --> 00:03:26.940
Balance.

00:03:27.490 --> 00:03:31.684
Balance: every class and every hard type must be represented.

00:03:31.984 --> 00:03:35.028
If 90% is easy, your accuracy lies.

00:03:35.578 --> 00:03:39.075
Count how many cases there are per class and per difficulty type.

00:03:39.375 --> 00:03:44.157
If ninety percent is easy, a system that solves only the easy part scores ninety and

00:03:44.157 --> 00:03:45.694
looks ready for production.

00:03:45.994 --> 00:03:50.798
Each class and each hard type needs enough representation for its hit rate to mean

00:03:50.798 --> 00:03:51.383
something.

00:03:51.683 --> 00:03:54.902
That implies over-representing rare cases on purpose.

00:03:55.202 --> 00:04:00.472
That's fine: an evaluation dataset doesn't aim to mirror production proportions, but

00:04:00.472 --> 00:04:02.542
to expose where the system fails.

00:04:03.842 --> 00:04:04.971
Step 5.

00:04:05.271 --> 00:04:06.376
Three splits.

00:04:06.926 --> 00:04:11.521
Split in three: dev to iterate, test never used for tuning nor quoted in prompt

00:04:11.521 --> 00:04:14.894
examples, and regression with everything that ever failed.

00:04:15.444 --> 00:04:16.921
Split the set in three.

00:04:17.221 --> 00:04:22.031
Dev is for iterating: there you try prompts and look at failures as often as you

00:04:22.031 --> 00:04:22.332
like.

00:04:22.632 --> 00:04:27.281
Test isn't used for tuning anything nor quoted in prompt examples, and is consulted

00:04:27.281 --> 00:04:28.625
only to make a decision.

00:04:28.925 --> 00:04:33.575
Regression collects everything that ever failed and got fixed, so it doesn't come

00:04:33.575 --> 00:04:33.862
back.

00:04:34.162 --> 00:04:36.730
Discipline with test is the hard part.

00:04:37.030 --> 00:04:41.730
Every time you adjust something while looking at those results, it stops measuring

00:04:41.730 --> 00:04:44.022
how the system will behave on new cases.

00:04:45.322 --> 00:04:46.509
Step 6.

00:04:46.809 --> 00:04:47.636
Versioning.

00:04:48.186 --> 00:04:50.604
Version as JSONL with a CHANGELOG.

00:04:50.904 --> 00:04:55.446
Fixing a label is a commit, and after a fix you recompute earlier metrics.

00:04:55.996 --> 00:05:01.217
Store each split as JSONL, one case per line with a stable identifier, and keep a

00:05:01.217 --> 00:05:01.861
CHANGELOG.

00:05:02.161 --> 00:05:06.135
Fixing a label is a commit with its reason, just like a code change.

00:05:06.435 --> 00:05:11.348
After a fix, earlier metrics must be recomputed, because the instrument changed and

00:05:11.348 --> 00:05:13.775
the old numbers are no longer comparable.

00:05:14.075 --> 00:05:16.782
Note the dataset version on each run card.

00:05:17.082 --> 00:05:21.531
Without that fact, a two-point improvement may be nothing more than a corrected

00:05:21.531 --> 00:05:21.868
label.

00:05:23.168 --> 00:05:24.274
An example.

00:05:24.824 --> 00:05:29.488
A team builds a dataset for a system that decides whether a new supplier already

00:05:29.488 --> 00:05:30.771
exists in the catalog.

00:05:31.071 --> 00:05:35.783
They gather one hundred and twenty cases: sixty from anonymized real usage, forty

00:05:35.783 --> 00:05:37.529
edge cases and twenty adverse.

00:05:37.829 --> 00:05:40.595
Two people label twenty and disagree on six.

00:05:40.895 --> 00:05:45.514
The origin is a single unanswered question: whether a branch counts as the same

00:05:45.514 --> 00:05:46.040
supplier.

00:05:46.340 --> 00:05:50.267
They decide it, add it to the guide and disagree on one in the second round.

00:05:50.567 --> 00:05:54.879
Counting, they discover only eight cases are of the "nearly identical name,

00:05:54.879 --> 00:05:57.466
different company" type, the costliest error.

00:05:57.766 --> 00:05:59.278
They add twelve more.

00:05:59.578 --> 00:06:03.342
They split into sixty dev, forty test and twenty regression.

00:06:04.642 --> 00:06:06.305
The most common mistakes.

00:06:06.855 --> 00:06:10.236
Letting the model generate the labels it's evaluated against.

00:06:10.786 --> 00:06:14.179
Labeling everything with a single person and no written guide.

00:06:14.729 --> 00:06:17.785
Tuning the prompt while looking at test set results.

00:06:18.335 --> 00:06:22.471
Fixing labels with no record and comparing old metrics with new ones.

00:06:23.771 --> 00:06:26.014
To know it is done, check this.

00:06:26.564 --> 00:06:29.934
The dataset has real, edge and adverse cases.

00:06:30.484 --> 00:06:33.668
There is a labeling guide with a rule for doubtful cases.

00:06:34.218 --> 00:06:37.541
Two people labeled a sample and resolved their disagreements.

00:06:38.091 --> 00:06:42.076
Dev, test and regression exist as JSONL with a CHANGELOG.

00:06:43.376 --> 00:06:45.271
And where AI fell short.

00:06:45.821 --> 00:06:49.388
A model graded against labels it generated always looks good.

00:06:49.688 --> 00:06:51.734
Test labels come from a person.

00:06:53.034 --> 00:06:54.720
That was day 17.

00:06:55.020 --> 00:06:59.994
The written guide, at gastongenaud.com, has the code to copy and the frequently

00:06:59.994 --> 00:07:01.001
asked questions.

00:07:01.552 --> 00:07:07.034
Continue with day 18: How to test a model: accuracy, N runs and ranges.
